WEBVTT

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.900
<b>Kansei University</b>

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:16.280
<b>Promises of Economic Cooperation</b>

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:16.990
<b>Boso Uni Wins First Half
Racing With Only 10 Members</b>

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:18.450
<b>Starting in last place
Brakes
Last place
Insufficient members</b>

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:20.410
<b>Inexperienced
Inadequate
Strengths and weaknesses</b>

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:22.870
<b>Difficult race</b>

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:26.960
Good morning.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:30.660
Go back to bed.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:35.810
Did it snow overnight?

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:39.410
We're all in the same boat.

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:41.270
Go to sleep.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.210
I'm worried.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:46.650
Will I trip on the slope?

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:49.550
Will my shoelaces break?

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:51.100
You'll be fine.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.760
You could even win your section.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:57.730
Can't you be more realistic
in your encouragement?

00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:01.960
You always do what you say you'll do.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:05.460
That goes for both the bar exam
and the Hakone Ekiden.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:07.920
The exam and the Ekiden are different.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:11.880
Tell me you'll try to win your section.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.020
Okay, okay.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:16.690
I'll go for it. I'll try to win.

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:18.240
I promise I'll try.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.810
Good. Then you'll be fine.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:25.770
If only I could've supported you
like this yesterday...

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:26.690
Huh?

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:30.750
I'm useless.

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:45.960
<b><i>Run with the Wind</i></b>

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:07.580
<i>There's a 60% chance of rain in Kanto,</i>

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:10.580
<b>Utsunomiya 4ºC/Cloudy
Yokohama 2ºC/Snow, cloudy later</b>

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:10.580
<b>Tokyo 3ºC/Snow, rain later
Kofu 1ºC/Snow</b>

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:10.580
<i>and it's snowing out on the plains.</i>

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:13.880
<i>The current temperature
in the city is 2º below zero.</i>

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:16.050
<i>There's a strong northeasterly wind,</i>

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:18.880
<i>and we expect temperatures
to remain below 5º this afternoon.</i>

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:22.430
Out for a jog? You're up early.

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:24.640
Where's Haiji?

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:27.310
In his room, probably. Why?

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:31.270
It's unusual for him not
to be running at this time.

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:32.860
Oh, right...

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:34.730
But he's Section 10.

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:38.970
Prince and Hana said 
they're getting ready right now.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:53.940
Hana?!

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:55.600
Wh-Who—

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:57.100
Who was that guy?!

00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:58.490
We're in a hotel!

00:03:58.490 --> 00:04:00.090
What are you talking about?

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:04.050
He just came out of Haiji's room.

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:05.230
What?

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:09.520
He's my sports doctor.

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:11.420
It's your leg, isn't it?

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:14.190
Did something happen?
How long has it been feeling off?

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.860
There's nothing wrong with it.
I was just being careful.

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:19.110
Haiji!

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:21.720
Focus on yourself.

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:24.370
Aren't you going to run the best you ever have?

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:32.250
<b><i>Goodbye, Beautiful World</i></b>

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:37.670
<b>New Year Sports Special
</b>Hakone Ekiden

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:37.670
<i>After finishing second in the first half,</i>

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:40.130
<b>Fujioka Kazuma</b>

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:40.130
<i>it's no surprise that Fujioka
Kazuma's running for Rikudo.</i>

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:43.380
<b>Kurahara Kakeru</b>

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:43.380
<i>They must be hoping their captain
will lead them to a comeback.</i>

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:44.880
Tokyo-Hakone Round-Trip College Ekiden Race
First Half Runners

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:47.520
Well, that's hardly surprising.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:49.200
I'll tell the others.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:50.200
Sure.

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.020
As far as Rikudo's concerned,
it's all going according to plan.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:58.900
Haiji and I will go to Tsurumi.

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:01.320
Kakeru and I will go to Totsuka.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:03.320
I'll be waiting at Otemachi.

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:06.060
I look forward to seeing you there.

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:12.700
<i>Nine,</i>

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:15.660
<b>Tokyo-Hakone Round-Trip College Ekiden Race</b>

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:15.660
<i>eight, seven, six,</i>

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:16.620
<i>five,</i>

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:17.580
<i>four,</i>

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:18.800
<i>three,</i>

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:20.500
<i>two, one.</i>

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:25.470
<b>Tokyo-Hakone Round-Trip College Ekiden Race</b>

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:26.940
<i>It's 8:00 AM. Boso Uni, who won
the first half, has started.</i>

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:31.200
<i>Second place finisher
Rikudo will start 1:39 later.</i>

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:32.890
Rikudo

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:34.920
<i>Universities who finished more
than ten minutes behind Boso</i>

00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:47.320
1 Boso
Rikudo
Daiwa
Kitakan
Kino
Saikyo
Do
Yokohama
Kofu Academy
Nihonbashi Tech
Tokyo Sport
Manaka
Teito
Ake
Jonan Bunka
Kanagawa
OP Student Association
Eurasia
Tokyo Academy
Shinsei
Kansei

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:37.790
<i>will be starting together
ten minutes from now.</i>

00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:42.370
<i>This year's simultaneous start
includes Eurasia, Tokyo Academy,</i>

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:47.320
<i>Shinsei, Kansei, and the
Kanto Student Association.</i>

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:51.780
We finished 11:53 behind Boso.

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:54.750
We may be starting simultaneously
ten minutes later,

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:57.790
but that doesn't erase the 1:53 difference.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:04.080
Our rank will ultimately be determined
by the sum of our first and second half times.

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:03.300
<b>Odahara Relay Point</b>

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:08.260
In other words, your apparent position
at the end of the second half

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:10.480
can be different from your time-based rank.

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:15.210
Members running the second half
have to do tedious calculations

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:17.370
while trying to improve their position.

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:22.690
<b>Tokyo-Hakone Round-Trip College Ekiden Race</b>

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:20.060
<i>The simultaneous start is soon!</i>

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:22.390
<i>All teams to the starting line!</i>

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:28.320
See you in Otemachi!

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:30.780
See you in Otemachi!

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:44.090
<i>The final five teams have started at once.</i>

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:49.090
<i>All 21 runners are now engaged
in the battle ending in Otemachi.</i>

00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:53.040
<i>They're speeding ahead.</i>

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:56.000
<i>Can they keep up that pace
with today's road conditions?</i>

00:06:56.390 --> 00:06:58.770
<b>Section 6 Final Entrants</b>

00:06:57.030 --> 00:06:58.770
Among Section 6's final entrants,

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:03.400
only Rikudo runs a 28-minute 10k.

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:05.690
You'll be able to keep up with them.

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:12.610
You're the only one 
who can ride down Hakone.

00:07:22.890 --> 00:07:24.420
<i>Don't be scared.</i>

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:28.470
Five kilometers in 13:45?

00:07:28.470 --> 00:07:29.470
That's too fast.

00:07:29.900 --> 00:07:35.240
But didn't Haiji say it's normal to run the
five kilometers of Section 6 in thirteen minutes?

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:37.540
If you're an experienced runner.

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:41.800
It's not easy to control your speed
on the downhill once you get going.

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:45.480
If you're not careful, you'll start
running at short-distance speeds.

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:49.610
Let's trust him.

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:52.390
All we can do is trust Yuki.

00:07:52.390 --> 00:07:55.670
Hana said the same thing: “Just trust him.”

00:07:56.030 --> 00:07:57.160
Hana did?

00:07:57.160 --> 00:07:58.620
Yeah. Hana—

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:00.700
Oh, uh...

00:08:01.010 --> 00:08:04.990
Hana said, uh... what was it?

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:07.260
We don't have to talk about it.

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:08.970
What does that mean?

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:12.170
H-Hey, listen.

00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:15.740
Just so you know, I like Hana.

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:17.510
Don't screw it up for me.

00:08:17.510 --> 00:08:18.760
I know.

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:19.770
What?

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:22.140
How do you know?!

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:35.160
<i>We just received the teams'
five-kilometer times.</i>

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:38.160
<b>New Years Sports Special</b>

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:38.160
<i>This is a rather fast pace. Right, Seto-san?</i>

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:42.160
<i>Yeah. When it comes to running
downhill, you need guts.</i>

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:45.360
Manaka and Kansei are on record pace.

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:47.240
We can't get a broadcast car to them yet.

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:48.730
Get a bike on Kansei.

00:08:48.730 --> 00:08:50.840
Follow them on the fixed cameras.

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:55.380
<i>Running downhill is like taking
your hands off the handlebars</i>

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:58.250
<i>while riding a bike down a steep slope.</i>

00:08:58.250 --> 00:09:01.060
<i>But if you have the courage and guts for it,</i>

00:09:01.060 --> 00:09:03.360
<i>you could go for the record.</i>

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:08.480
<i>Manaka's Fudemura and Kansei's
Iwakura are running particularly quickly.</i>

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:18.240
<i>These past five kilometers,
I've run each kilometer in 2:45.</i>

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:19.610
<i>It's unbelievable.</i>

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:21.870
<i>I could never run this fast on a flat track.</i>

00:09:22.210 --> 00:09:25.330
<i>No one can run that fast.</i>

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:27.860
<i>There is one.</i>

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:30.140
<i>Oh, right.</i>

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:32.710
<i>This is the world Kakeru runs in.</i>

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:34.800
<i>Kakeru...</i>

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:38.460
<i>Do you always run this fast?</i>

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:47.110
<i>If I keep running like this, can I be like you?</i>

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:52.110
<i>No, don't!</i>

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:54.750
<i>It's too dangerous for me.</i>

00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:57.570
<i>Today's my last day.</i>

00:09:57.890 --> 00:10:00.410
<i>I have my own life to live.</i>

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:04.530
<i>Hey, Kakeru...</i>

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:07.280
<i>Don't go too far.</i>

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:12.730
<i>The place you're going is
beautiful, but it's too lonely.</i>

00:10:13.390 --> 00:10:17.750
<i>Too lonely for any living human to endure.</i>

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:25.010
<i>Shoot.</i>

00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:27.680
<i>Focus. Focus, focus.</i>

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:31.490
Yukihiko! Yukihiko!

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:31.490
Brother!

00:10:31.490 --> 00:10:33.550
You can do it!

00:10:35.620 --> 00:10:38.320
Yukihiko!

00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:50.960
Yukihiko, I'm thinking
about getting remarried.

00:10:56.100 --> 00:11:02.350
And you're going to have a little sister.

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:16.910
<i>I'm sorry,</i>

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:19.850
Mom...

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:27.250
Yuki!

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:41.680
I knew you'd do it!

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:47.600
Yuki!

00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:49.710
That was amazing!

00:11:49.710 --> 00:11:51.190
You're amazing, Yuki!

00:11:51.190 --> 00:11:53.560
Two seconds faster, and you would've won!

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:55.100
Just two seconds!

00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:57.600
Two seconds?

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:00.640
That's amazing! You're amazing, Yuki!

00:12:02.540 --> 00:12:06.220
That two-second gap will never close.

00:12:18.990 --> 00:12:20.180
It's me.

00:12:20.180 --> 00:12:23.130
Are you calling to tell me
Section 7's strategy?

00:12:23.130 --> 00:12:26.090
No. Just run to your heart's content.

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:27.800
That's all.

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:29.490
That's simple.

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:31.770
<i>Keep a pace of three minutes per kilometer.</i>

00:12:31.770 --> 00:12:33.320
<i>That would be ideal.</i>

00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:35.070
That won't be easy.

00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:36.480
Roger that.

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:39.500
<i>Sorry I couldn't let you take it easy.</i>

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:44.450
Haiji, if I wanted to take it easy, 
I wouldn't have run.

00:12:45.110 --> 00:12:47.410
<i>The same goes for dieting
and quitting smoking.</i>

00:12:48.130 --> 00:12:50.070
There's no such thing as an easy path.

00:12:50.070 --> 00:12:52.200
I knew that when I made my decision.

00:12:52.850 --> 00:12:53.880
<i>I understand.</i>

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:56.720
Watch me. Just don't expect too much.

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:01.710
<i>Must be nice...</i>

00:13:02.510 --> 00:13:03.840
<i>...being so skinny.</i>

00:13:04.630 --> 00:13:09.470
<i>If I'd been born that skinny, I—wait...</i>

00:13:09.860 --> 00:13:11.720
<i>I caught up?</i>

00:13:11.720 --> 00:13:13.340
<i>Maybe he's off his game.</i>

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:15.260
<i>What position am I? What's my time?</i>

00:13:17.170 --> 00:13:19.400
<i>I screwed up. What should I do?</i>

00:13:19.400 --> 00:13:21.150
<i>Should I match his pace?</i>

00:13:22.850 --> 00:13:25.720
<i>Of course he won't make it easy.</i>

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:28.320
<i>Fine, then. Let's do this.</i>

00:13:36.550 --> 00:13:39.210
<i>Damn, this is fun.</i>

00:13:39.210 --> 00:13:43.160
<i>This is it. This is how I wanted to run.</i>

00:13:43.590 --> 00:13:45.560
Run to your heart's content.

00:13:47.700 --> 00:13:51.220
<i>Haiji, you truly are amazing.</i>

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:54.480
Hirata,

00:13:55.320 --> 00:13:58.840
why don't you give up on
long-distance and try something else?

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:03.320
I think you'd be better at a throwing event.

00:14:06.590 --> 00:14:08.360
<i>It's not his fault.</i>

00:14:09.220 --> 00:14:11.450
<i>He said that because he'd been
observing me carefully.</i>

00:14:12.130 --> 00:14:16.250
<i>Since the numbers decide the race,
it's only natural that body type matters.</i>

00:14:17.240 --> 00:14:21.670
<i>But I liked running.</i>

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:26.000
<i>I wanted to keep running if I could,</i>

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:28.830
<i>but...</i>

00:14:30.900 --> 00:14:32.450
<i>I lost my passion.</i>

00:14:40.910 --> 00:14:41.920
Hey.

00:14:42.640 --> 00:14:44.500
You're not a child anymore.

00:14:44.500 --> 00:14:46.450
Smoke if you want to.

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:51.820
You used to run track?

00:14:54.330 --> 00:14:56.500
A long time ago.

00:14:56.500 --> 00:14:58.660
In high school? What events?

00:14:59.090 --> 00:15:00.590
I don't remember anymore.

00:15:00.590 --> 00:15:02.900
Why? Are you a track fan?

00:15:04.240 --> 00:15:05.460
I was a runner.

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:07.170
What?

00:15:07.700 --> 00:15:09.870
Long-distance, throughout
middle and high school.

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:12.440
Then why'd you choose Kansei?

00:15:12.440 --> 00:15:14.730
This isn't a school for running.

00:15:14.730 --> 00:15:16.640
Why? You slow or something?

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:20.100
Not slow, just weak.

00:15:20.460 --> 00:15:21.560
What?

00:15:22.420 --> 00:15:24.630
I'm weak.

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:28.740
<i>You're not the slightest bit weak.</i>

00:15:29.050 --> 00:15:33.940
<i>No, you've spent four years getting stronger.</i>

00:15:34.460 --> 00:15:37.830
<i>Nicotine! Can you hear me, Nicotine?</i>

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:40.910
<i>That's just my addiction, old man.</i>

00:15:41.790 --> 00:15:45.680
<i>You're currently in 13th place.</i>

00:15:45.680 --> 00:15:51.260
<i>When you include Kansei's first half time,
 your current total position is</i>

00:15:51.260 --> 00:15:52.620
<i>16th place.</i>

00:15:57.260 --> 00:16:03.270
<b>Hiratsuka Relay Station</b>

00:15:57.620 --> 00:16:00.010
<i>Even after how fast Yuki ran?</i>

00:16:02.060 --> 00:16:05.270
This year will be your last year at Hakone.

00:16:07.340 --> 00:16:10.280
After all, you won't earn a seed.

00:16:10.780 --> 00:16:12.900
Isn't that good for you, though?

00:16:12.900 --> 00:16:15.550
You don't have to worry next year.

00:16:16.050 --> 00:16:18.050
You think we'll take that lying—

00:16:18.050 --> 00:16:19.580
You don't know that.

00:16:19.580 --> 00:16:23.340
Tokyo Sport's barely in range for a seed, too.

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:26.260
I'll pass them all.

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:30.830
I'll crush everyone in front of me.

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:37.550
I've been running since middle school,
believing that I'd be standing here.

00:16:38.110 --> 00:16:41.140
You run for fun. You wouldn't understand.

00:16:41.140 --> 00:16:42.310
It's not for fun!

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:48.380
Yesterday, our teammate practically gave
his life to pass along our sash.

00:16:48.380 --> 00:16:50.580
Who suffers that much for fun?

00:16:50.580 --> 00:16:53.450
You should know that, but you...

00:16:54.010 --> 00:16:57.400
Play fair and square.
Show how strong you are by running.

00:16:57.400 --> 00:16:58.660
Musa.

00:17:00.020 --> 00:17:02.260
Sakaki, what are you doing?

00:17:04.120 --> 00:17:06.130
Start by trying to have fun.

00:17:06.130 --> 00:17:10.090
Our Kiyose tells us all the time
that just being fast isn't enough.

00:17:10.520 --> 00:17:12.010
Lip service.

00:17:12.500 --> 00:17:14.220
Winning is everything.

00:17:20.850 --> 00:17:22.150
<i>There's no one here.</i>

00:17:22.710 --> 00:17:24.220
<i>There's no target.</i>

00:17:25.310 --> 00:17:29.140
<i>I don't know where or how I'm running.</i>

00:17:29.140 --> 00:17:31.070
<i>I don't know if I'm fast or slow.</i>

00:17:31.070 --> 00:17:32.400
<i>I don't know anything.</i>

00:17:32.980 --> 00:17:34.900
Keep a pace of three minutes per kilometer.

00:17:34.900 --> 00:17:36.150
That would be ideal.

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:40.240
Thanks for letting me run again.

00:17:40.240 --> 00:17:42.240
But that means you were frustrated, right?

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:44.290
That's why you told me.

00:17:44.650 --> 00:17:46.710
It means you can't give it up.

00:17:47.720 --> 00:17:50.710
<i>Thanks for telling me the truth.</i>

00:17:50.710 --> 00:17:52.630
Tenth, Kansei University.

00:17:51.500 --> 00:17:52.630
<b>Kansei University</b>

00:17:52.630 --> 00:17:54.420
Yes!

00:17:55.380 --> 00:17:57.930
<i>Thanks for running with me.</i>

00:17:59.140 --> 00:18:00.830
<i>This is my last day.</i>

00:18:01.260 --> 00:18:02.930
<i>This world is too beautiful.</i>

00:18:03.820 --> 00:18:05.530
<i>But that's okay.</i>

00:18:05.530 --> 00:18:09.310
<i>My love hasn't changed. 
Never has, never will.</i>

00:18:10.790 --> 00:18:12.320
<i>I like running.</i>

00:18:13.590 --> 00:18:14.700
<i>I love it.</i>

00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:22.640
<i>I'll leave all my lingering
attachments on this road!</i>

00:18:30.390 --> 00:18:33.400
Yeah! Nico-chan Senpai!

00:18:33.400 --> 00:18:35.150
Let's do this!

00:18:38.990 --> 00:18:40.390
Don't lose.

00:18:47.420 --> 00:18:48.740
You improved our rank!

00:18:48.740 --> 00:18:51.340
You improved our rank, Nico-chan Senpai!

00:18:56.080 --> 00:18:57.630
Tastes like iron.

00:18:58.320 --> 00:19:03.280
<b>1 Boso
2 Rikudo</b>

00:18:58.660 --> 00:19:00.700
<i>Boso remains in first place.</i>

00:19:00.700 --> 00:19:02.910
<i>They've pulled ahead of Rikudo</i>

00:19:02.910 --> 00:19:07.130
<i>and, once again, achieved greater than 
a 1:30 lead by the Hiratsuka Relay Station.</i>

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:18.390
Even for Rikudo, not everything
goes according to plan.

00:19:19.150 --> 00:19:22.820
There's no fun in running a race
when you know how it ends,

00:19:22.820 --> 00:19:27.310
but there is an end I'm determined to reach.

00:19:28.160 --> 00:19:32.020
Kurahara, I'm going to break the section record.

00:19:35.740 --> 00:19:38.370
We're obliged to win.

00:19:38.920 --> 00:19:43.080
Athletes, contacts, coaches, facilities, funds.

00:19:43.520 --> 00:19:47.580
Champions in everything, we're expected to win.

00:19:48.600 --> 00:19:50.760
Do you think it's hard?

00:19:50.760 --> 00:19:53.450
You get used to the difficulty, eventually.

00:19:53.450 --> 00:19:56.590
But it's heavy. So heavy.

00:19:56.980 --> 00:20:02.590
The past four years, I've endured
that weight and ran with it...

00:20:03.920 --> 00:20:05.550
...to become even stronger.

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:10.480
Then I'll break your record.

00:20:10.970 --> 00:20:15.840
You'll probably only be the
record-holder for about ten minutes.

00:20:18.570 --> 00:20:20.610
Rikudo will win!

00:20:21.140 --> 00:20:26.120
At the same time, I will beat
myself and beat you, Kurahara.

00:20:27.010 --> 00:20:30.250
What will you show me?

00:20:34.160 --> 00:20:35.640
Kakeru!

00:20:38.260 --> 00:20:39.680
King is...

00:20:40.840 --> 00:20:44.500
<i>Tokyo Sport first-year Sakaki Kosuke
puts on a tremendous burst of speed.</i>

00:20:44.500 --> 00:20:49.320
<i>He's pulling ahead of Kansei, who passed
their sash before them at Hiratsuka.</i>

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:51.410
<i>What a striking debut for this rookie.</i>

00:20:52.970 --> 00:20:55.660
Damn! Damn, damn!

00:20:56.020 --> 00:20:57.980
Damn, damn!

00:20:57.980 --> 00:20:59.980
Damn, damn!

00:22:40.590 --> 00:22:43.250
Next time, “Embrace Your Loneliness.”

00:22:40.710 --> 00:22:46.020
<b>Next time: Embrace Your Loneliness</b>
